<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; background-color: #e0f2f7; border: 2px solid #1a5276; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Personal Goals for the Future Include...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a diary entry about a significant event. Interactive: Collaborate in writing, with guidance, on a shared text for a group project.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a diary entry about future academic goals (e.g., "I have to send my application to the university"). Can collaborate with peers to write about plans for high school, using a structured outline with clear steps and future goals (e.g., "First, we will complete our science classes next, we will graduate with a Bachillerato en Ciencias to apply for university"). Can collaborate on a group project outline for future studies (e.g., "First, I have to research careers, then, I have to make a plan").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 40%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 45%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and grammar for future plans.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a picture of a student looking at a university brochure and another of someone working in a specific career (e.g., engineer, doctor). Students complete the sentence "In the future, I want to..." on a small sticky note and place it on the board. This activates prior knowledge and connects to future goals, preparing for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes two sentences on the board about their own future academic or career goals using target grammar. E.g., "I <b>have to</b> learn new skills to achieve my <b>career goals</b>." "In five years, I <b>will be developing</b> new educational <b>opportunities</b>." The teacher models the pronunciation of "have to" and "will be working," emphasizing clear articulation, and explicitly points out the use of 'have to' for necessity and 'will be' for future continuous actions. They also highlight the minimal pair words "math" and "mass" on the board, enunciating clearly for /θ/ and /s/ sounds, then ask students to repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I <b>have to</b> study for the test,' does it mean it's optional or necessary?" (Expected answer: Necessary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Next year, I <b>will be studying</b> abroad,' am I talking about the past, present, or future?" (Expected answer: Future)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content related to "Future Plans and Goals" through an authentic written text, consolidating understanding of vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post or an excerpt from a student's diary entry (narrative register) about their future plans after high school. The text explicitly uses "have to," "must," future continuous, WH-questions for goals, and vocabulary such as <i>future, goals, plan, career, opportunity, achieve, prepare, ambitious, important</i>. For example:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Dear Diary, Today I started thinking about my <b>future</b>. It's so <b>important</b> to have a good <b>plan</b>. I <b>have to</b> study hard for my final exams. My main <b>goal</b> is to get into a good university. I want to be an engineer, so I <b>must</b> focus on <b>math</b> and science. In five years, I <b>will be studying</b> engineering and gaining experience. I really hope to <b>achieve</b> this <b>ambitious</b> dream. <b>What</b> do I need to <b>prepare</b> for this <b>career opportunity</b>?</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding short descriptive texts about personal goals and future plans, identifying key information about academic and career development.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the diary entry individually. Then, they answer the following questions in writing in their notebooks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the student's main goal for the future?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does the student "have to" do to achieve this goal?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What will the student "be doing" in five years?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing. Students practice forming sentences and short paragraphs using target grammar and vocabulary about future plans and goals.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences and short paragraphs about future plans and goals using modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), WH-questions, future continuous, and specified vocabulary (e.g., <i>future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, develop, ambitious, important</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences about future plans, each missing a target grammar structure or vocabulary word. Students complete the gaps. E.g., "I _____ (must/have to) finish school to get a good _____ (career/business)." "In three years, I _____ (be working/will be working) on my own _____ (plan/development)." (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> Students rewrite sentences to include either "have to," "must," or a future continuous form. For example, "It is necessary for me to save money for university." becomes "I <b>have to</b> save money for university." Or, "I will be studying English next year." becomes a question: "<b>What</b> will you <b>be studying</b> next year?" They also practice using specific vocabulary in their new sentences. (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Writing (Sentence Chain):</b> In pairs, students create a "Future Plans Chain." Student A writes a sentence about a future goal (e.g., "I <b>plan to</b> study <b>business</b>."). Student B then adds a sentence using a modal of necessity or future continuous, continuing the thought (e.g., "To do that, I <b>must</b> learn about <b>development</b>. In five years, I <b>will be preparing</b> my own <b>opportunity</b>."). They take turns, writing at least 4-5 sentences each, ensuring correct grammar and integrating target vocabulary like <i>ambitious, important, vision</i>. They then peer-check each other's sentences for accuracy in grammar, spelling, and vocabulary use. (7 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic writing scenario, focusing on fluency and communicative task completion related to "Future Plans and Goals."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Project Outline Writing & Individual Diary Entry.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. Each group will collaboratively write a structured outline for their plans for high school and future studies. The outline should include clear steps and future goals, using phrases like "First, we will...", "Next, we have to...", "After that, we plan to...". They must incorporate modals of necessity ("have to," "must"), future continuous, and at least five target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>future, goals, plan, career, achieve, develop</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Following the group activity, each student will individually write a short diary entry (3-5 sentences) about one specific personal academic goal for the future. The entry must use "I have to..." or "I must..." and at least one future continuous sentence (e.g., "In a few years, I will be working on..."), explicitly stating a goal and a necessary step to achieve it. They should refer to their individual "vision" and "ambitious" aspirations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher monitors, providing guidance and offering suggestions, focusing on the content and use of the target language rather than strict grammatical perfection at this stage. Students are encouraged to engage in sociolinguistic discussions during the collaborative writing, offering feedback and suggestions to each other.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection on the writing task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their individual diary entries with a partner. Using a simple peer assessment checklist, they check for:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the entry include a future academic goal? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the entry use "have to" or "must" correctly? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the entry use future continuous correctly? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the writing clear and understandable? (Yes/No/Needs work)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did it use at least two new vocabulary words from the lesson? (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher collects the group project outlines to review for use of target grammar and vocabulary, organization, and collaborative effort.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b> Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>goals, plan, career</i>), correct application of "have to," "must," and future continuous, clarity of written expression, and effective collaboration in group writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one new word or grammar point you used in your writing today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did you express your future goals in writing? (1-5 stars)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and forward planning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did I find challenging about writing about my future goals today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one thing I will do to improve my English writing skills about future plans?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The collaborative writing activity worked well, fostering good peer interaction and application of "have to" for practical steps. Students demonstrated understanding of future continuous, but some still confused "have to" with simple future "will." I need to provide more scaffolded examples emphasizing the necessity aspect of "have to" versus simple prediction. The pronunciation of minimal pairs like /θ/ and /s/ was briefly touched upon; for future lessons, I could integrate a quick dictation exercise using these words to connect spelling to sound in written work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their collaboratively written group project outlines and individual diary entries to prepare a short presentation or poster summarizing their future academic and career plans, mediating information between written plans and oral presentation. This will integrate the written work into a larger 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>